The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (!timing_np) {
    pr_err("%pOF: could not find node '%s'\n", np, name);
}
```
Reason:
The unconditional error message was printed when the timing node was not found, which was misleading in scenarios where the absence of the node was acceptable due to a fallback mechanism being available. This created unnecessary clutter in the logs, indicating an error when there was none.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (!timing_np)
    return -ENOENT;
```
Reason:
By removing the error message and returning `-ENOENT` when the timing node is not found, the code now allows for cases where a fallback is acceptable without logging an error. This change reduces confusion and keeps the logs cleaner, while still allowing for error reporting if there are actual issues with parsing the timings when the node is present.

